1
00:01:46,273 --> 00:01:47,617
Dr. Redding.

2
00:01:47,641 --> 00:01:49,652
I didn't expect to see
you for another two weeks.

3
00:01:49,676 --> 00:01:50,787
How was Rio?

4
00:01:50,811 --> 00:01:52,489
Door open.

5
00:01:52,513 --> 00:01:54,490
<i>Certainly, Dr. Redding.</i>

6
00:02:00,454 --> 00:02:03,032
Door open.

7
00:02:03,056 --> 00:02:06,102
♪ And many more ♪

8
00:02:06,126 --> 00:02:08,004
Happy Birthday, Vanessa.

9
00:02:08,028 --> 00:02:10,807
Door open, override,
clearance one.

10
00:02:10,831 --> 00:02:12,776
Oh, Vincent, darling.

11
00:02:12,800 --> 00:02:14,010
You're back.

12
00:02:14,034 --> 00:02:16,112
Just in time to join us.

13
00:02:16,136 --> 00:02:19,649
Do you realize what would
happen to this institute

14
00:02:19,673 --> 00:02:23,353
if one of the clients paying
for your alleged genius

15
00:02:23,377 --> 00:02:24,921
came in here and
saw you behaving

16
00:02:24,945 --> 00:02:26,189
like emotional adolescents?

17
00:02:26,213 --> 00:02:29,392
Actually, your wife's
birthday gave us an excuse

18
00:02:29,416 --> 00:02:31,494
to sample something made with my

19
00:02:31,518 --> 00:02:34,764
biovidasucranouse
flower substitute.

20
00:02:34,788 --> 00:02:37,400
I want to see the reports
on the current status

21
00:02:37,424 --> 00:02:40,937
of each of your projects
on my desk in two hours.

22
00:02:40,961 --> 00:02:41,971
Door open.

23
00:02:41,995 --> 00:02:43,915
<i>Certainly, Dr. Redding.</i>

24
00:03:38,852 --> 00:03:39,962
Ah! Don't hurt me.

25
00:03:39,986 --> 00:03:41,897
Irwin.

26
00:03:41,921 --> 00:03:42,932
Dr. Redding.

27
00:03:42,956 --> 00:03:44,900
What the hell are
you doing here?

28
00:03:44,924 --> 00:03:47,937
I'm retrieving this .38
caliber firing mechanism

29
00:03:47,961 --> 00:03:50,139
for the velocity
test I'm conducting.

30
00:03:50,163 --> 00:03:52,442
Uh, what are you
doing here, sir?

31
00:04:00,073 --> 00:04:01,918
Someone let a man
in through that door.

32
00:04:01,942 --> 00:04:03,152
I followed him in here.

33
00:04:03,176 --> 00:04:05,021
So that's what that was.

34
00:04:05,045 --> 00:04:07,290
I thought something
suspicious was going on.

35
00:04:07,314 --> 00:04:08,391
You saw them?

36
00:04:08,415 --> 00:04:10,393
I saw a man I didn't recognize.

37
00:04:10,417 --> 00:04:11,961
Why didn't you challenge him?

38
00:04:11,985 --> 00:04:13,996
It seemed potentially dangerous.

39
00:04:14,020 --> 00:04:17,100
Irwin, you had a gun.

40
00:04:18,692 --> 00:04:22,005
Oh, that's right.

41
00:04:22,029 --> 00:04:23,739
Only one of you
four senior people

42
00:04:23,763 --> 00:04:26,609
could have opened that door.
No one else has the combination.

43
00:04:26,633 --> 00:04:30,146
Vincent, surely you don't
think one of us is responsible?

44
00:04:30,170 --> 00:04:31,214
Oh, yes. He does.

45
00:04:31,238 --> 00:04:32,415
This is insulting.

46
00:04:32,439 --> 00:04:34,817
I am changing the
combination to that door.

47
00:04:34,841 --> 00:04:37,487
No one will have
access to it but me.

48
00:04:37,511 --> 00:04:39,021
I suggest you get
back to your work,

49
00:04:39,045 --> 00:04:41,558
and having read
your status reports,

50
00:04:41,582 --> 00:04:43,359
I mean that sincerely.

51
00:04:43,383 --> 00:04:44,716
Door open.

52
00:04:49,790 --> 00:04:52,435
Don't forget: dinner
tonight with the Jarvicks.

53
00:04:52,459 --> 00:04:54,137
Irwin.

54
00:04:54,161 --> 00:04:55,471
Yes, sir?

55
00:04:55,495 --> 00:04:57,940
When did I tell you your
project report was due?

56
00:04:57,964 --> 00:05:00,610
Uh, when you came back from Rio.

57
00:05:00,634 --> 00:05:04,948
Well, I'm back, and I
want it on my desk by 5:30.

58
00:05:04,972 --> 00:05:06,316
Door closed.

59
00:05:06,340 --> 00:05:07,917
<i>Door closes.</i>

60
00:05:07,941 --> 00:05:09,952
But he came back early.

61
00:05:09,976 --> 00:05:12,955
I can't get two weeks
of work done in one day.

62
00:05:12,979 --> 00:05:16,659
Irwin, I think he knows that.

63
00:05:16,683 --> 00:05:17,843
In fact, he's counting on it.

64
00:05:19,285 --> 00:05:21,931
He's just looking for an
excuse to fire me, isn't he?

65
00:05:21,955 --> 00:05:23,922
Brilliant deduction.

66
00:05:25,425 --> 00:05:28,438
He can't do this to me.

67
00:05:28,462 --> 00:05:30,873
What can you do about it?

68
00:05:53,120 --> 00:05:54,586
Dr. Redding.

69
00:05:55,989 --> 00:05:58,501
My project report,
sir, on your de...

70
00:06:06,800 --> 00:06:07,966
Huh.

71
00:06:22,482 --> 00:06:23,459
Phone.

72
00:06:23,483 --> 00:06:24,827
Dial.

73
00:06:25,853 --> 00:06:29,832
Uh, 555-9930.

74
00:06:33,526 --> 00:06:35,138
<i>Ben Matlock's office.</i>

75
00:06:35,162 --> 00:06:36,138
Yes, hello.

76
00:06:36,162 --> 00:06:37,941
I have to speak
with Ben Matlock.

77
00:06:37,965 --> 00:06:39,375
It's urgent.

78
00:06:39,399 --> 00:06:40,899
Tell him it's his nephew.

79
00:06:44,905 --> 00:06:46,816
Ben Matlock for Irwin Bruckner.

80
00:06:46,840 --> 00:06:48,807
Just a moment.

81
00:06:50,376 --> 00:06:53,077
Uh, that's doctor.

82
00:06:54,214 --> 00:06:56,459
Dr. Irwin Bruckner.

83
00:06:57,751 --> 00:06:59,662
He's my nephew.

84
00:06:59,686 --> 00:07:01,364
Okay, and you are?

85
00:07:01,388 --> 00:07:03,333
Ben Matlock.

86
00:07:05,225 --> 00:07:09,861
Uh, th... There's no doctor
or anything, just Ben Matlock.

87
00:07:13,333 --> 00:07:14,666
I am a lawyer.

88
00:07:16,903 --> 00:07:18,148
Okay.

89
00:07:18,172 --> 00:07:19,882
Wear this at all times.

90
00:07:19,906 --> 00:07:22,718
Go to the door.

91
00:07:22,742 --> 00:07:24,753
Face it.

92
00:07:24,777 --> 00:07:27,923
Say your name.

93
00:07:27,947 --> 00:07:29,192
Ben Matlock.

94
00:07:29,216 --> 00:07:31,494
All right, you're in
the computer memory.

95
00:07:31,518 --> 00:07:34,363
Everything here
is voice activated.

96
00:07:34,387 --> 00:07:38,000
First address the object, then
tell it what you want it to do.

97
00:07:38,024 --> 00:07:39,457
Door open.

98
00:07:42,395 --> 00:07:44,329
And it will open? Yes.

99
00:07:45,999 --> 00:07:48,110
All I have to do is say
that and it will open?

100
00:07:48,134 --> 00:07:49,445
Yes.

101
00:07:49,469 --> 00:07:51,147
Well, why didn't it
open when you said it?

102
00:07:51,171 --> 00:07:53,082
I have a different
voice command.

103
00:07:53,106 --> 00:07:55,685
So all I do... Just
say, door open.

104
00:07:55,709 --> 00:07:57,420
Door open.

105
00:07:57,444 --> 00:07:59,888
<i>Welcome to the Mansbridge
Institute, Mr. Matlock.</i>

106
00:07:59,912 --> 00:08:02,413
<i>Dr. Bruckner is expecting you.</i>

107
00:08:15,896 --> 00:08:18,997
<i>Now proceed to the
third door on your left.</i>

108
00:08:36,016 --> 00:08:37,493
Irwin!

109
00:08:37,517 --> 00:08:38,894
Door open.

110
00:08:38,918 --> 00:08:40,752
Override clearance six.

111
00:08:43,790 --> 00:08:44,967
Irwin?

112
00:08:44,991 --> 00:08:46,657
Oh, thank God you're here.

113
00:08:47,994 --> 00:08:50,074
Are you in some kind of trouble?

114
00:08:57,804 --> 00:08:59,215
It's Vincent Redding.

115
00:08:59,239 --> 00:09:01,484
He runs... He ran this place.

116
00:09:01,508 --> 00:09:03,886
I came in here to give
him my project report.

117
00:09:03,910 --> 00:09:05,376
This is how I found him.

118
00:09:06,446 --> 00:09:08,624
Are the police on the way?

119
00:09:08,648 --> 00:09:10,759
No.

120
00:09:10,783 --> 00:09:12,194
You didn't call the police?

121
00:09:12,218 --> 00:09:14,664
Uncle Ben, no one
can get in this office

122
00:09:14,688 --> 00:09:17,133
unless someone on
the inside admits them.

123
00:09:17,157 --> 00:09:18,734
And he was dead?

124
00:09:20,260 --> 00:09:21,859
And no one else in the room.

125
00:09:25,331 --> 00:09:27,691
Irwin, we have
to call the police.

126
00:09:28,601 --> 00:09:30,112
Okay.

127
00:09:30,136 --> 00:09:32,014
Phone, dial security.

128
00:09:33,039 --> 00:09:34,584
<i>Security.</i>

129
00:09:34,608 --> 00:09:35,852
This is Dr. Bruckner.

130
00:09:35,876 --> 00:09:37,787
Dr. Redding has been shot.

131
00:09:37,811 --> 00:09:39,789
He appears to be dead.

132
00:09:39,813 --> 00:09:41,290
You better call the police.

133
00:09:41,314 --> 00:09:42,314
<i>Okay, right away.</i>

134
00:09:43,516 --> 00:09:45,527
Everybody is gonna
think there's just no way

135
00:09:45,551 --> 00:09:46,829
I could have gotten in here

136
00:09:46,853 --> 00:09:49,632
unless Redding
was alive to let me in.

137
00:09:49,656 --> 00:09:53,269
Well, I got in.

138
00:09:53,293 --> 00:09:55,171
I was able to command
the door to open

139
00:09:55,195 --> 00:09:56,839
by overriding the system.

140
00:09:56,863 --> 00:09:59,542
Maybe the killer
overrode the system.

141
00:09:59,566 --> 00:10:02,245
I've been over every
inch of this room.

142
00:10:02,269 --> 00:10:03,313
Nobody hiding.

143
00:10:03,337 --> 00:10:05,248
Nobody going out the window.

144
00:10:05,272 --> 00:10:06,782
Nobody's voice
on a tape recorder

145
00:10:06,806 --> 00:10:08,985
that could have
activated the door.

146
00:10:09,009 --> 00:10:10,086
Nothing.

147
00:10:10,110 --> 00:10:13,222
Well, uh, somebody
had to have opened

148
00:10:13,246 --> 00:10:16,392
that door for you
by remote control.

149
00:10:16,416 --> 00:10:18,160
That just isn't possible.

150
00:10:18,184 --> 00:10:20,162
Only the computer
can open the door.

151
00:10:20,186 --> 00:10:22,131
And only someone on
the inside of an office

152
00:10:22,155 --> 00:10:24,533
can order the computer.

153
00:10:24,557 --> 00:10:28,270
If I didn't know better,
I'd assume I shot him too.

154
00:10:28,294 --> 00:10:32,441
Oh. At least your fingerprints

155
00:10:32,465 --> 00:10:36,145
aren't all over the
murder weapon.

156
00:10:36,169 --> 00:10:37,513
You're kidding.

157
00:10:37,537 --> 00:10:40,449
I'm almost positive
that's the gun

158
00:10:40,473 --> 00:10:43,819
I was using last night in
my velocity experiments.

159
00:10:43,843 --> 00:10:49,392
Somebody must have
retrieved it from the supply room.

160
00:10:49,416 --> 00:10:52,828
Well, it's still not
an airtight case.

161
00:10:52,852 --> 00:10:54,351
You didn't have a motive.

162
00:10:56,289 --> 00:10:57,400
You're kidding.

163
00:10:57,424 --> 00:11:00,269
I think he was just
about to fire me.

164
00:11:00,293 --> 00:11:02,204
You must have killed him.

165
00:11:02,228 --> 00:11:03,439
No, I didn't.

166
00:11:03,463 --> 00:11:05,908
I swear to you,
Uncle Ben, I didn't.

167
00:11:06,933 --> 00:11:08,544
Police. Open up.

168
00:11:08,568 --> 00:11:12,003
Uh, door open,
override clearance six.

169
00:11:14,507 --> 00:11:16,285
Uh, come... Come
right in, gentlemen.

170
00:11:16,309 --> 00:11:17,309
That's Dr. Redding.

171
00:11:18,778 --> 00:11:20,389
I believe he's dead.

172
00:11:20,413 --> 00:11:24,260
You'll note, uh, two bullet
holes in his chest, and, uh...

173
00:11:24,284 --> 00:11:26,604
You'd better let
me do the talking.

174
00:11:36,763 --> 00:11:39,509
Maybe it was suicide.

175
00:11:39,533 --> 00:11:41,544
Maybe Dr. Redding shot himself,

176
00:11:41,568 --> 00:11:43,312
and then, with his dying breath,

177
00:11:43,336 --> 00:11:45,615
commanded the door
to open to admit Irwin.

178
00:11:45,639 --> 00:11:49,707
I'd sure hate to have to explain
how he shot himself twice.

179
00:11:53,746 --> 00:11:56,158
Somebody had to figure out

180
00:11:56,182 --> 00:11:58,227
some way to fool
the computer system.

181
00:11:58,251 --> 00:11:59,295
That's all there is to it.

182
00:11:59,319 --> 00:12:00,551
Yeah.

183
00:12:01,721 --> 00:12:02,698
Irwin.

184
00:12:02,722 --> 00:12:03,899
What?

185
00:12:03,923 --> 00:12:06,001
Any idea how somebody
could have figured out

186
00:12:06,025 --> 00:12:08,203
a way to fool the
computer system?

187
00:12:08,227 --> 00:12:11,107
Uh, no, not really.

188
00:12:11,131 --> 00:12:12,842
Uh, okay. All right.

189
00:12:16,169 --> 00:12:17,513
We agree it was
a frame, don't we?

190
00:12:17,537 --> 00:12:19,281
Yeah.

191
00:12:19,305 --> 00:12:22,151
Oh.

192
00:12:22,175 --> 00:12:26,288
Uh, there are three people
who could have set up the frame.

193
00:12:26,312 --> 00:12:28,257
All three knew that Dr. Redding

194
00:12:28,281 --> 00:12:30,459
was expecting Irwin
in his office that night,

195
00:12:30,483 --> 00:12:32,723
and all three were
coworkers of Irwin's.

196
00:12:34,120 --> 00:12:36,465
Well, the institute was
just like any other business

197
00:12:36,489 --> 00:12:38,768
out to make a profit.

198
00:12:38,792 --> 00:12:41,192
What about competitors
or business rivals?

199
00:12:42,829 --> 00:12:44,073
Hey, Irwin.

200
00:12:44,097 --> 00:12:45,741
Huh? Uh,

201
00:12:45,765 --> 00:12:49,511
who were Mansbridge's
business rivals?

202
00:12:49,535 --> 00:12:51,247
Business rivals.

203
00:12:51,271 --> 00:12:55,051
Uh, none of any real importance.

204
00:12:55,075 --> 00:12:58,887
Uh, something strange did
happen the day before the murder.

205
00:12:58,911 --> 00:13:01,424
I went down to the storage
room to get that gun,

206
00:13:01,448 --> 00:13:04,393
and one of my colleagues
snuck an unauthorized person

207
00:13:04,417 --> 00:13:06,862
into the building.

208
00:13:06,886 --> 00:13:07,863
Oh.

209
00:13:07,887 --> 00:13:09,431
Oh.

210
00:13:09,455 --> 00:13:11,367
Who? I don't know.

211
00:13:11,391 --> 00:13:12,835
I only saw him for a moment.

212
00:13:12,859 --> 00:13:14,337
Oh.

213
00:13:14,361 --> 00:13:16,839
Well, give Cassie the
best description you can.

214
00:13:16,863 --> 00:13:17,873
We'll get Tyler in on this.

215
00:13:17,897 --> 00:13:19,408
Okay.

216
00:13:19,432 --> 00:13:21,165
Okay. Go ahead.

217
00:13:30,443 --> 00:13:32,654
Uh, are you sure I'm
not disturbing you?

218
00:13:32,678 --> 00:13:36,225
I assure you you're
not disturbing me at all.

219
00:13:36,249 --> 00:13:40,830
Although, uh, I must warn
you, it's almost time for my nap.

220
00:13:40,854 --> 00:13:44,956
Oh, I think grabbing a few winks
during the day is a great idea.

221
00:13:47,093 --> 00:13:48,804
I take five naps a day

222
00:13:48,828 --> 00:13:51,106
at regularly
scheduled intervals.

223
00:13:51,130 --> 00:13:53,676
The alarm goes
off, I fall asleep.

224
00:13:53,700 --> 00:13:56,745
Ten minutes later, it
goes off again. I wake up.

225
00:13:56,769 --> 00:13:58,414
Alert, refreshed,

226
00:13:58,438 --> 00:14:01,851
and capable of working
virtually around the clock.

227
00:14:01,875 --> 00:14:03,853
What happens if
the alarm goes off

228
00:14:03,877 --> 00:14:06,155
and you don't feel
like going to asleep?

229
00:14:06,179 --> 00:14:11,059
My mind and body do what they've
been trained to do, Mr. Matlock.

230
00:14:12,285 --> 00:14:14,652
It's just a matter
of conditioning.

231
00:14:16,389 --> 00:14:20,869
But... I digress.

232
00:14:20,893 --> 00:14:22,638
What did you want to ask me?

233
00:14:22,662 --> 00:14:25,941
Any idea who killed
Vincent Redding?

234
00:14:25,965 --> 00:14:31,514
Heh. Well, it all boils down to
one simple fact, Mr. Matlock.

235
00:14:31,538 --> 00:14:33,783
Irwin could not have
entered that office

236
00:14:33,807 --> 00:14:37,019
unless Dr. Redding
was alive to admit him.

237
00:14:37,043 --> 00:14:40,655
I-Is...? Is there n-no way

238
00:14:40,679 --> 00:14:43,559
somebody could
sidestep the system?

239
00:14:43,583 --> 00:14:45,327
Absolutely not.

240
00:14:45,351 --> 00:14:48,664
I mean, you're
all geniuses here.

241
00:14:48,688 --> 00:14:50,165
In other words,

242
00:14:50,189 --> 00:14:53,435
who else would have wanted
to kill Vincent Redding?

243
00:14:53,459 --> 00:14:54,459
Yeah.

244
00:14:56,395 --> 00:14:57,762
All of us.

245
00:14:58,831 --> 00:15:00,197
All of us hated him.

246
00:15:01,201 --> 00:15:04,168
Myself, Vanessa, Paul Loo.

247
00:15:05,572 --> 00:15:07,349
Now that Vincent's dead,

248
00:15:07,373 --> 00:15:11,008
Paul Loo could very
well wind up with his job.

249
00:15:12,078 --> 00:15:13,856
Oh.

250
00:15:13,880 --> 00:15:15,724
Oh.

251
00:15:15,748 --> 00:15:20,129
Do you know if Paul
Loo was in Vincent's office

252
00:15:20,153 --> 00:15:22,865
between 4:30 and 5:30
the day of the murder?

253
00:15:22,889 --> 00:15:26,235
No. I'm afraid I was
in my office, working.

254
00:15:26,259 --> 00:15:27,891
I didn't see anyone.

255
00:15:29,662 --> 00:15:30,728
Well...

256
00:15:32,799 --> 00:15:35,166
Uh... Uh...

257
00:15:54,154 --> 00:15:55,197
He does that.

258
00:15:55,221 --> 00:15:57,199
He's done it to me
dozens of times.

259
00:15:57,223 --> 00:16:01,770
Just dozes off in
mid-sentence and he snores.

260
00:16:02,929 --> 00:16:03,994
I'm sorry.

261
00:16:05,065 --> 00:16:06,097
Here. Try some.

262
00:16:07,166 --> 00:16:08,743
Well, thanks.

263
00:16:08,767 --> 00:16:11,680
Uh, I'm kind of hoping

264
00:16:11,704 --> 00:16:16,251
that you can help me and
my client out here, Dr. Loo.

265
00:16:16,275 --> 00:16:18,087
Wish I could. I like Irwin.

266
00:16:18,111 --> 00:16:21,690
But I'm afraid I was in here
working when Vincent was killed.

267
00:16:21,714 --> 00:16:23,292
I'm no help at all.

268
00:16:23,316 --> 00:16:26,128
Hm, taste good.

269
00:16:26,152 --> 00:16:28,097
Made them myself.

270
00:16:28,121 --> 00:16:29,531
The ultimate junk food.

271
00:16:29,555 --> 00:16:34,103
No salt, no fat, no sugar,
no calories and no food value.

272
00:16:34,127 --> 00:16:35,938
What's in 'em?

273
00:16:35,962 --> 00:16:36,972
Not much.

274
00:16:36,996 --> 00:16:38,373
Hm.

275
00:16:38,397 --> 00:16:40,075
Tastes good.

276
00:16:40,099 --> 00:16:41,676
If the Food and
Drug Administration

277
00:16:41,700 --> 00:16:44,679
weren't so hysterical
for possible health risks,

278
00:16:44,703 --> 00:16:47,582
I would have had them
on the market years ago.

279
00:16:47,606 --> 00:16:49,206
Here, have some more.

280
00:16:52,979 --> 00:16:55,290
Mm.

281
00:16:55,314 --> 00:16:56,480
If...

282
00:17:00,620 --> 00:17:05,234
If it were possible to
circumvent the system,

283
00:17:05,258 --> 00:17:08,738
and if somebody besides
Irwin killed Dr. Redding,

284
00:17:08,762 --> 00:17:11,707
any likely suspects
come to mind?

285
00:17:11,731 --> 00:17:12,875
Any one of us.

286
00:17:12,899 --> 00:17:14,209
We all hated him.

287
00:17:14,233 --> 00:17:17,513
Dr. Barnes, myself,
his wife, Vanessa.

288
00:17:17,537 --> 00:17:21,250
He treated her no different
than he treated everyone else.

289
00:17:21,274 --> 00:17:22,274
Like dirt?

290
00:17:23,443 --> 00:17:26,422
Actually, he
treated dirt better.

291
00:17:26,446 --> 00:17:27,578
Mm-hm.

292
00:17:29,682 --> 00:17:31,927
Somebody said
she's so grief-stricken

293
00:17:31,951 --> 00:17:34,885
she can't talk on the
phone or work or anything.

294
00:17:39,525 --> 00:17:40,591
Hm.

295
00:17:53,606 --> 00:17:54,984
<i>Yes, who is it?</i>

296
00:17:55,008 --> 00:17:56,118
Michelle Thomas.

297
00:17:56,142 --> 00:17:57,820
I'm an attorney
for Irwin Bruckner.

298
00:17:57,844 --> 00:17:59,355
I'd like to speak
to Dr. Segwick.

299
00:18:01,314 --> 00:18:03,159
I'm Dr. Segwick.

300
00:18:03,183 --> 00:18:05,349
I trust this won't take long.

301
00:18:07,420 --> 00:18:09,665
I'm so sorry to bother you.

302
00:18:09,689 --> 00:18:11,767
I know your husband's death

303
00:18:11,791 --> 00:18:14,737
came as a terrible blow.

304
00:18:14,761 --> 00:18:17,907
My exercise physiologist.

305
00:18:17,931 --> 00:18:20,642
Please, just get to the point.

306
00:18:20,666 --> 00:18:21,710
Of course.

307
00:18:21,734 --> 00:18:22,978
I found your husband's passport

308
00:18:23,002 --> 00:18:24,313
when I went through his effects,

309
00:18:24,337 --> 00:18:28,317
and I wondered, if he'd
just gotten back from Brazil,

310
00:18:28,341 --> 00:18:30,219
why hadn't it been stamped?

311
00:18:30,243 --> 00:18:31,320
Oh, really.

312
00:18:31,344 --> 00:18:32,721
Wouldn't it be more logical

313
00:18:32,745 --> 00:18:35,023
to ask the customs
authorities that question?

314
00:18:35,047 --> 00:18:38,883
Well, it's just that you're
here and customs is there.

315
00:18:39,986 --> 00:18:41,496
I have no notion

316
00:18:41,520 --> 00:18:43,531
why they didn't
stamp his passport.

317
00:18:43,555 --> 00:18:45,322
Perhaps they ran out of ink.

318
00:18:46,492 --> 00:18:48,370
Your office is
like Dr. Redding's.

319
00:18:48,394 --> 00:18:50,939
Do you know of any
quirks or malfunctions

320
00:18:50,963 --> 00:18:52,241
that could explain how...

321
00:18:52,265 --> 00:18:56,378
Your client murdered my
husband, Miss Thomas.

322
00:18:56,402 --> 00:18:59,447
Vincent was alive when
Dr. Bruckner entered his office.

323
00:18:59,471 --> 00:19:00,971
I heard him admit him.

324
00:19:02,342 --> 00:19:03,652
You what?

325
00:19:03,676 --> 00:19:05,954
I was on the phone with Vincent.

326
00:19:05,978 --> 00:19:09,792
He commanded the door to
open, said, "Hello, Dr. Bruckner,"

327
00:19:09,816 --> 00:19:11,615
and told me he'd call me back.

328
00:19:12,685 --> 00:19:13,963
He never did.

329
00:19:13,987 --> 00:19:16,431
Why didn't you tell
anybody this before?

330
00:19:16,455 --> 00:19:19,234
Because, quite
frankly, I assumed Irwin

331
00:19:19,258 --> 00:19:21,203
would hire himself
a competent attorney

332
00:19:21,227 --> 00:19:25,440
who would, in time, find out
about it and plead him guilty.

333
00:19:25,464 --> 00:19:28,932
We don't believe
Dr. Bruckner is guilty.

334
00:19:30,503 --> 00:19:33,282
We think it was someone
else at the institute

335
00:19:33,306 --> 00:19:34,883
who killed your husband.

336
00:19:34,907 --> 00:19:36,573
Really?

337
00:19:44,817 --> 00:19:46,883
Perhaps Dr. Barnes.

338
00:19:48,320 --> 00:19:50,232
He was being considered
for a higher-paying job

339
00:19:50,256 --> 00:19:51,900
at the Raleigh Foundation.

340
00:19:51,924 --> 00:19:53,935
Vincent got wind of it,

341
00:19:53,959 --> 00:19:56,972
said the wrong things
to the right people,

342
00:19:56,996 --> 00:19:58,774
and put the kibosh on it.

343
00:19:58,798 --> 00:20:01,699
When Jeremy heard
about it, he was livid.

344
00:20:03,269 --> 00:20:04,680
Well, you'll have to excuse me.

345
00:20:04,704 --> 00:20:07,015
My heart rate
is falling rapidly,

346
00:20:07,039 --> 00:20:08,716
my patience is wearing thin

347
00:20:08,740 --> 00:20:10,585
and I am in mourning.

348
00:20:54,153 --> 00:20:55,530
That's him, huh?

349
00:20:55,554 --> 00:20:57,766
That's as close
as we're gonna get.

350
00:20:57,790 --> 00:20:59,668
That is amazing.

351
00:20:59,692 --> 00:21:00,769
What is?

352
00:21:00,793 --> 00:21:02,805
Irwin. He accessed
the police mainframe

353
00:21:02,829 --> 00:21:04,539
and used their
composite sketch software

354
00:21:04,563 --> 00:21:06,308
to get a picture of the
guy in the storage room.

355
00:21:06,332 --> 00:21:07,309
No kidding?

356
00:21:07,333 --> 00:21:08,410
Hot off the press.

357
00:21:08,434 --> 00:21:10,545
Oh, that's great.

358
00:21:10,569 --> 00:21:12,214
Call Tyler.

359
00:21:12,238 --> 00:21:13,615
Tell him to go
down to the institute,

360
00:21:13,639 --> 00:21:15,617
take this with him,
keep his eyes open.

361
00:21:15,641 --> 00:21:18,487
Dr. Redding never changed
the combination on that door.

362
00:21:18,511 --> 00:21:19,854
Maybe that guy
will show up again.

363
00:21:19,878 --> 00:21:20,856
Do it.

364
00:21:20,880 --> 00:21:22,120
What's next for me?

365
00:21:23,516 --> 00:21:26,428
You never did figure
out just how somebody

366
00:21:26,452 --> 00:21:28,196
opened Dr. Redding's
door for you?

367
00:21:28,220 --> 00:21:30,766
I still don't see
how it's possible.

368
00:21:30,790 --> 00:21:31,766
I'll keep working on it.

369
00:21:31,790 --> 00:21:33,156
Yeah. Yeah.

370
00:21:34,360 --> 00:21:35,360
That's right.

371
00:21:36,762 --> 00:21:39,508
Uh, any...? Any, uh, messages?

372
00:21:39,532 --> 00:21:40,976
Just one from Michelle.

373
00:21:41,000 --> 00:21:43,511
She's following up on a
lead she got from Dr. Segwick.

374
00:21:43,535 --> 00:21:44,780
Good.

375
00:21:44,804 --> 00:21:47,049
He's kind of cute, isn't he?

376
00:21:47,073 --> 00:21:48,050
Who?

377
00:21:48,074 --> 00:21:50,251
Irwin. Irwin?

378
00:21:50,275 --> 00:21:52,253
Uh, I know he's a
genius and everything,

379
00:21:52,277 --> 00:21:55,190
but he's like a kid.
He's kind of cute.

380
00:23:55,735 --> 00:23:57,768
I'm sorry, babe.

381
00:24:26,999 --> 00:24:28,832
Virgin Mary, please.

382
00:24:36,843 --> 00:24:37,953
Thanks.

383
00:25:26,859 --> 00:25:28,291
Thanks.

384
00:26:55,848 --> 00:26:57,425
Hi.

385
00:26:57,449 --> 00:26:58,682
Hi.

386
00:27:02,387 --> 00:27:03,553
How's it going?

387
00:27:05,724 --> 00:27:07,302
I think I'm making
some progress.

388
00:27:07,326 --> 00:27:09,171
You see, what I've done
is I've written a program

389
00:27:09,195 --> 00:27:12,207
which allows me to recalculate
the response to each allophone

390
00:27:12,231 --> 00:27:13,641
using simple mathematics.

391
00:27:13,665 --> 00:27:16,678
For instance, in this equation,
each of the gamma of ends

392
00:27:16,702 --> 00:27:18,447
represents the allophone
speech sequence.

393
00:27:18,471 --> 00:27:20,148
We transform it
into a predictive code

394
00:27:20,172 --> 00:27:21,771
for the security program.

395
00:27:44,396 --> 00:27:48,409
The guy Irwin saw
showed up at the institute.

396
00:27:48,433 --> 00:27:49,744
What was he doing there?

397
00:27:49,768 --> 00:27:52,881
Just as I was about
to go in, he came out,

398
00:27:52,905 --> 00:27:54,750
so I follow him to
Fenwick's Tavern,

399
00:27:54,774 --> 00:27:58,654
where he met with a man
wearing a very expensive suit

400
00:27:58,678 --> 00:28:00,644
and he gave him some papers.

401
00:28:01,647 --> 00:28:02,847
Who was the man in the suit?

402
00:28:03,882 --> 00:28:05,560
He left before I could find out.

403
00:28:05,584 --> 00:28:07,083
Who was the other guy?

404
00:28:08,354 --> 00:28:11,866
Uh, he left before
I could find out.

405
00:28:11,890 --> 00:28:13,835
They both left before
you could find out?

406
00:28:13,859 --> 00:28:14,936
Yeah. You lost 'em.

407
00:28:14,960 --> 00:28:17,172
Yeah. You said
you had good news.

408
00:28:17,196 --> 00:28:20,875
The guy Irwin saw drives
a very expensive sports car,

409
00:28:20,899 --> 00:28:22,544
and there's only three
places in this town

410
00:28:22,568 --> 00:28:24,746
that does body
work on cars like that.

411
00:28:24,770 --> 00:28:27,290
And guess who gave
it a great big dent?

412
00:28:33,712 --> 00:28:35,757
I called him from my
office to remind him

413
00:28:35,781 --> 00:28:37,826
that we had a dinner
engagement that night,

414
00:28:37,850 --> 00:28:39,828
and while we were talking,
he said, "Door open,"

415
00:28:39,852 --> 00:28:42,330
and, uh, "Hello, Dr. Bruckner."

416
00:28:42,354 --> 00:28:44,565
Which led you to conclude what?

417
00:28:44,589 --> 00:28:47,602
That he had just admitted
Irwin Bruckner to his office.

418
00:28:47,626 --> 00:28:50,505
Thank you, Dr. Segwick.

419
00:28:50,529 --> 00:28:52,329
Nothing further.

420
00:28:53,398 --> 00:28:54,642
Your witness.

421
00:28:54,666 --> 00:28:57,912
Thank you. Uh, Dr. Segwick,

422
00:28:57,936 --> 00:29:01,916
your husband had just
returned from a trip to where?

423
00:29:01,940 --> 00:29:03,617
Brazil.

424
00:29:03,641 --> 00:29:04,953
Rio de Janeiro.

425
00:29:04,977 --> 00:29:07,388
And he left on the 8th

426
00:29:07,412 --> 00:29:10,191
and was supposed to, uh,

427
00:29:10,215 --> 00:29:12,427
come back on the 29th.

428
00:29:12,451 --> 00:29:13,561
Is that right?

429
00:29:13,585 --> 00:29:15,697
Yes, but he came
back early on the 14th.

430
00:29:15,721 --> 00:29:17,565
He must have missed you.

431
00:29:17,589 --> 00:29:19,901
More likely he missed his work.

432
00:29:19,925 --> 00:29:23,071
Well... Did you drive
him to the airport?

433
00:29:23,095 --> 00:29:25,615
He drove his car
and left it there.

434
00:29:26,798 --> 00:29:28,810
Dr. Segwick,

435
00:29:28,834 --> 00:29:31,913
this is a gasoline
credit-card receipt

436
00:29:31,937 --> 00:29:34,849
dated the 13th,

437
00:29:34,873 --> 00:29:37,419
from the Richmond Center
gas station up in Richmond.

438
00:29:37,443 --> 00:29:39,323
Do you recognize your
husband's signature?

439
00:29:41,513 --> 00:29:44,380
Yes, that's his signature,
but it must be misdated.

440
00:29:45,750 --> 00:29:47,870
Well, I suppose
that could happen.

441
00:29:48,787 --> 00:29:52,433
Uh, this is an official

442
00:29:52,457 --> 00:29:54,135
United States passport

443
00:29:54,159 --> 00:29:57,806
issued to Dr. Vincent Redding.

444
00:29:57,830 --> 00:29:59,741
You recognize it?

445
00:29:59,765 --> 00:30:01,376
Yes, that's Vincent's.

446
00:30:01,400 --> 00:30:05,247
There is no passport
stamp whatsoever

447
00:30:05,271 --> 00:30:08,939
for the period between the
8th and the 14th, is there?

448
00:30:10,642 --> 00:30:12,120
No.

449
00:30:12,144 --> 00:30:16,391
That's because Vincent
Redding was not in Rio de Janeiro.

450
00:30:16,415 --> 00:30:20,295
He was at the Jimmy
Nash Clinic in Richmond,

451
00:30:20,319 --> 00:30:22,652
being treated for acute
depression, wasn't he?

452
00:30:26,559 --> 00:30:27,891
Yes.

453
00:30:28,960 --> 00:30:30,905
If it's ruled

454
00:30:30,929 --> 00:30:33,074
that your husband
was not killed,

455
00:30:33,098 --> 00:30:36,644
but rather, committed suicide,

456
00:30:36,668 --> 00:30:39,180
there will be no
insurance proceeds.

457
00:30:39,204 --> 00:30:41,182
You'll not be able to collect

458
00:30:41,206 --> 00:30:44,352
on your husband's
$1-million insurance policy,

459
00:30:44,376 --> 00:30:48,723
because suicide was
specifically excluded

460
00:30:48,747 --> 00:30:50,491
in that policy, wasn't it?

461
00:30:50,515 --> 00:30:52,126
I suppose so, but...

462
00:30:52,150 --> 00:30:54,129
You made up that story
about the phone call

463
00:30:54,153 --> 00:30:56,531
to further incriminate
Irwin Bruckner,

464
00:30:56,555 --> 00:30:58,933
in an attempt to make
sure no one would suspect

465
00:30:58,957 --> 00:31:01,936
your husband committed
suicide, didn't you?

466
00:31:01,960 --> 00:31:04,271
He didn't commit suicide.

467
00:31:04,295 --> 00:31:07,141
Maybe not. Maybe.

468
00:31:07,165 --> 00:31:10,811
You did make up
that story, perjury,

469
00:31:10,835 --> 00:31:13,214
trying to eliminate
the slightest chance

470
00:31:13,238 --> 00:31:17,452
that your husband's death
could be ruled suicide,

471
00:31:17,476 --> 00:31:18,476
didn't you?

472
00:31:22,381 --> 00:31:23,447
Yes.

473
00:31:26,651 --> 00:31:28,351
No further questions.

474
00:31:32,958 --> 00:31:34,903
Uncle Ben...
Uncle Ben, I did it.

475
00:31:34,927 --> 00:31:35,970
I did it.

476
00:31:35,994 --> 00:31:37,372
I-I was gonna take
a walk, you know,

477
00:31:37,396 --> 00:31:38,740
just to try and clear my head,

478
00:31:38,764 --> 00:31:40,341
and just as I was
going out the door,

479
00:31:40,365 --> 00:31:43,478
Cassie says, "I-It looks like
rain, you'd better take this."

480
00:31:43,502 --> 00:31:44,479
And then it hit me.

481
00:31:44,503 --> 00:31:46,381
The umbrella?

482
00:31:46,405 --> 00:31:47,415
The operative theory.

483
00:31:47,439 --> 00:31:49,150
When it rains,
everything gets wet,

484
00:31:49,174 --> 00:31:50,518
but when it rains

485
00:31:50,542 --> 00:31:52,787
and you stand under an
umbrella, you don't get wet.

486
00:31:52,811 --> 00:31:55,289
I-It's raining, yet
it's not raining.

487
00:31:55,313 --> 00:31:57,391
The operations
program was functioning,

488
00:31:57,415 --> 00:31:59,527
yet it was not functioning,

489
00:31:59,551 --> 00:32:00,728
because another program,

490
00:32:00,752 --> 00:32:02,864
for the most part
identical to the original,

491
00:32:02,888 --> 00:32:05,266
was in simultaneous operation,

492
00:32:05,290 --> 00:32:07,802
acting, uh, kind
of like an umbrella,

493
00:32:07,826 --> 00:32:09,070
and at predetermined points,

494
00:32:09,094 --> 00:32:11,614
it would take over
the original program.

495
00:32:13,498 --> 00:32:14,898
Incredible.

496
00:32:17,036 --> 00:32:22,149
This program will
show that it was possible

497
00:32:22,173 --> 00:32:25,887
for someone besides
Dr. Redding to open that door?

498
00:32:25,911 --> 00:32:27,555
Absolutely.

499
00:32:27,579 --> 00:32:32,448
I-It will when and,
uh, if I can access it.

500
00:32:33,718 --> 00:32:37,298
You know it's in the computer

501
00:32:37,322 --> 00:32:39,834
at the institute.

502
00:32:39,858 --> 00:32:41,169
You just don't know where?

503
00:32:41,193 --> 00:32:42,313
Right.

504
00:32:44,329 --> 00:32:46,841
Maybe I can explain
your theory to the judge.

505
00:32:46,865 --> 00:32:48,576
We have to have proof, Irwin.

506
00:32:48,600 --> 00:32:50,712
Uh, okay.

507
00:32:50,736 --> 00:32:53,080
Um, well, maybe I could, uh,

508
00:32:53,104 --> 00:32:55,717
access the mainframe
at the institute from here,

509
00:32:55,741 --> 00:32:57,451
uh, bring in a few
more terminals,

510
00:32:57,475 --> 00:32:59,955
uh, get a couple of buddies,
work around the clock...

511
00:33:02,080 --> 00:33:03,190
But the odds of coming across

512
00:33:03,214 --> 00:33:05,159
that access code are slim.

513
00:33:05,183 --> 00:33:06,961
How slim?

514
00:33:06,985 --> 00:33:11,254
Roughly 4,185,000-to-1.

515
00:33:12,591 --> 00:33:15,436
Maybe you'll get lucky.

516
00:33:18,429 --> 00:33:21,075
Ben Matlock's office.

517
00:33:21,099 --> 00:33:23,011
It's Tyler. He's
at the body shop.

518
00:33:23,035 --> 00:33:25,113
Says he just got lucky.

519
00:33:39,518 --> 00:33:40,561
May I help you?

520
00:33:40,585 --> 00:33:42,096
Yes. I'm looking
for Douglas Harding.

521
00:33:42,120 --> 00:33:44,632
I have it on good
authority he works here.

522
00:33:44,656 --> 00:33:46,267
Douglas Harding.

523
00:33:46,291 --> 00:33:47,368
I'm kind of new here.

524
00:33:47,392 --> 00:33:49,337
I'll have to check.
Just a minute.

525
00:33:51,329 --> 00:33:52,707
Excuse me, sir.

526
00:33:52,731 --> 00:33:55,443
There's someone out here
asking for Douglas Harding?

527
00:33:55,467 --> 00:33:57,412
Uh, Ben Matlock.
Is that Mr. Drake?

528
00:33:57,436 --> 00:34:00,070
He says his name
is Ben Matlock, sir.

529
00:34:01,406 --> 00:34:03,885
I see. Thank you.

530
00:34:03,909 --> 00:34:06,287
Apparently, uh, Douglas
Harding did work here,

531
00:34:06,311 --> 00:34:08,022
but he quit a year ago.

532
00:34:08,046 --> 00:34:09,690
Oh.

533
00:34:09,714 --> 00:34:11,258
This the way to
Mr. Drake's office?

534
00:34:11,282 --> 00:34:14,128
You can't go in there.

535
00:34:34,673 --> 00:34:36,539
Come in, Mr. Matlock.

536
00:34:38,543 --> 00:34:40,488
Mr. Harding.

537
00:34:40,512 --> 00:34:42,190
Mr. Drake.

538
00:34:42,214 --> 00:34:43,591
How can we help you?

539
00:34:43,615 --> 00:34:46,861
Everything okay, Mr. Drake?

540
00:34:46,885 --> 00:34:49,405
Thank you, John,
everything's fine.

541
00:34:51,790 --> 00:34:53,301
Anything else?

542
00:34:53,325 --> 00:34:55,703
Uh, an employee of mine

543
00:34:55,727 --> 00:34:58,306
saw Mr. Harding here,

544
00:34:58,330 --> 00:35:00,374
uh, hand you an envelope,

545
00:35:00,398 --> 00:35:02,610
uh, some sort of papers,

546
00:35:02,634 --> 00:35:05,413
at Fenwick's Tavern.

547
00:35:10,542 --> 00:35:13,254
But what I wanna know
is why your employee,

548
00:35:13,278 --> 00:35:14,722
Mr. Harding,

549
00:35:14,746 --> 00:35:18,659
was seen sneaking in and
out of Mansbridge Institute?

550
00:35:18,683 --> 00:35:20,594
Well, you'd have to ask him.

551
00:35:20,618 --> 00:35:21,596
Mr. Harding?

552
00:35:21,620 --> 00:35:23,464
I believe you have me confused

553
00:35:23,488 --> 00:35:24,568
with someone else.

554
00:35:26,758 --> 00:35:29,537
Your company has
defense contracts, doesn't it?

555
00:35:29,561 --> 00:35:31,638
Yes. And testing done at places

556
00:35:31,662 --> 00:35:33,507
such as Mansbridge Institute

557
00:35:33,531 --> 00:35:35,977
could affect your
business greatly?

558
00:35:36,001 --> 00:35:37,378
Of course.

559
00:35:37,402 --> 00:35:39,514
Now, what's your
point, Mr. Matlock?

560
00:35:39,538 --> 00:35:44,018
Uh, my point is that I'm not
mistaken about Mr. Harding.

561
00:35:44,042 --> 00:35:47,955
He was seen sneaking
into Mansbridge Institute.

562
00:35:47,979 --> 00:35:50,124
And it doesn't take
a genius to figure out

563
00:35:50,148 --> 00:35:52,593
that maybe some
money changed hands,

564
00:35:52,617 --> 00:35:55,763
and maybe some test results

565
00:35:55,787 --> 00:35:59,355
were falsified to benefit you.

566
00:36:00,559 --> 00:36:03,838
But that's not what
this is all about.

567
00:36:03,862 --> 00:36:06,340
Vincent Redding
has been murdered,

568
00:36:06,364 --> 00:36:11,979
so we're not talking about
money or defense contracts.

569
00:36:12,003 --> 00:36:14,282
It's very possible,

570
00:36:14,306 --> 00:36:16,784
if Dr. Redding's death
has anything to do

571
00:36:16,808 --> 00:36:19,219
with Mr. Harding here,

572
00:36:19,243 --> 00:36:21,622
the two of you might wind up

573
00:36:21,646 --> 00:36:24,825
doing quite a bit
of time for murder.

574
00:36:24,849 --> 00:36:27,795
So that's what
I'm talking about.

575
00:36:27,819 --> 00:36:29,664
And I would strongly
urge you to tell me

576
00:36:29,688 --> 00:36:32,533
who you're dealing
with down at the institute.

577
00:36:57,416 --> 00:36:59,093
I guess I don't have to ask

578
00:36:59,117 --> 00:37:00,995
if Irwin's figured
out the program.

579
00:37:01,019 --> 00:37:02,963
No. I've never seen
anything like this.

580
00:37:02,987 --> 00:37:04,632
Oh, neither have
I. It's beyond me.

581
00:37:04,656 --> 00:37:05,633
Yes.

582
00:37:05,657 --> 00:37:06,734
Whoosh.

583
00:37:07,992 --> 00:37:09,069
Irwin's smart.

584
00:37:09,093 --> 00:37:10,605
I had no idea.

585
00:37:10,629 --> 00:37:17,044
I mean, smart... on some things,
and on others, he's really dumb.

586
00:37:17,068 --> 00:37:18,779
He's a kid.

587
00:37:18,803 --> 00:37:21,415
Oh, yeah. Yeah.

588
00:37:21,439 --> 00:37:23,150
It's funny, isn't it,

589
00:37:23,174 --> 00:37:25,455
who a person will
be attractive to.

590
00:37:26,811 --> 00:37:29,022
Oh, I'm sorry.

591
00:37:29,046 --> 00:37:31,726
I've seen you looking at
him, and you said he was cute.

592
00:37:31,750 --> 00:37:33,794
Well, he is. He's cute.

593
00:37:33,818 --> 00:37:37,632
Yeah, his mother used
to say that all the time.

594
00:37:37,656 --> 00:37:39,100
She'd say, "Irwin's cute."

595
00:37:39,124 --> 00:37:40,890
He's very bright.

596
00:37:41,959 --> 00:37:44,472
He's strange, but he's cute.

597
00:37:44,496 --> 00:37:45,939
He is.

598
00:37:45,963 --> 00:37:47,141
Which one?

599
00:37:47,165 --> 00:37:48,176
All of them.

600
00:37:48,200 --> 00:37:49,610
Strange.

601
00:37:49,634 --> 00:37:50,811
Strange.

602
00:37:50,835 --> 00:37:52,413
Uncle Ben!

603
00:37:52,437 --> 00:37:54,715
I got it!

604
00:37:56,908 --> 00:38:00,388
Dr. Segwick,
uh, is it fair to say

605
00:38:00,412 --> 00:38:02,590
that you're an
expert in physics?

606
00:38:02,614 --> 00:38:04,925
Yes, I think that would be fair.

607
00:38:04,949 --> 00:38:08,395
This was subpoenaed
from the physics lab

608
00:38:08,419 --> 00:38:11,132
at Mansbridge Institute
just this morning.

609
00:38:11,156 --> 00:38:12,933
Please tell the
court what it is.

610
00:38:12,957 --> 00:38:14,969
It's a sensor.

611
00:38:14,993 --> 00:38:17,805
It's used to measure
microfracture formation rates.

612
00:38:17,829 --> 00:38:20,208
And you use this
device in your research

613
00:38:20,232 --> 00:38:21,909
at the institute, don't you?

614
00:38:21,933 --> 00:38:24,345
Yes. I've been working
with it for several months.

615
00:38:24,369 --> 00:38:27,782
This device will be used
in the courtroom today,

616
00:38:27,806 --> 00:38:30,184
and what I'd like,
uh, for you to do

617
00:38:30,208 --> 00:38:32,287
is just compare it with this one

618
00:38:32,311 --> 00:38:35,356
that was loaned to us
from the physics department

619
00:38:35,380 --> 00:38:36,957
at the university.

620
00:38:36,981 --> 00:38:38,447
It's the same sensor.

621
00:38:39,417 --> 00:38:40,561
In every way?

622
00:38:47,492 --> 00:38:48,725
Except...

623
00:38:49,794 --> 00:38:52,506
the bias resistor
has been replaced.

624
00:38:52,530 --> 00:38:54,942
The value's all wrong.

625
00:38:54,966 --> 00:38:57,578
That throws off the
calibration by a factor of ten.

626
00:38:57,602 --> 00:38:59,580
You seem alarmed.

627
00:38:59,604 --> 00:39:01,082
Of course I'm alarmed.

628
00:39:01,106 --> 00:39:03,351
This sensor is
from the institute.

629
00:39:03,375 --> 00:39:04,985
It means that the
results of any tests

630
00:39:05,009 --> 00:39:07,788
that I ran using it
are totally invalid.

631
00:39:07,812 --> 00:39:14,028
Say someone were
testing an alloy.

632
00:39:14,052 --> 00:39:17,131
If that person were
using this device,

633
00:39:17,155 --> 00:39:18,866
would those tests
be invalid also?

634
00:39:18,890 --> 00:39:20,601
Of course they would.

635
00:39:20,625 --> 00:39:22,103
Thank you, doctor.

636
00:39:26,731 --> 00:39:29,243
I'd have to check my records
to be absolutely certain,

637
00:39:29,267 --> 00:39:32,146
but yes, this could very well
be the sensor I've been using.

638
00:39:32,170 --> 00:39:33,414
Okay.

639
00:39:33,438 --> 00:39:36,117
Now, correct me if I'm wrong,

640
00:39:36,141 --> 00:39:39,453
but that would make
the alloy you're testing

641
00:39:39,477 --> 00:39:44,158
appear to be ten times
stronger than it actually is.

642
00:39:44,182 --> 00:39:46,182
Unfortunately, yes.

643
00:39:47,251 --> 00:39:49,096
Who paid you to do these tests?

644
00:39:49,120 --> 00:39:50,831
Uh, Drake Industries.

645
00:39:50,855 --> 00:39:52,166
Drake Industries. That's...

646
00:39:52,190 --> 00:39:54,868
That's the company that
developed the alloy, right?

647
00:39:54,892 --> 00:39:56,737
That's right.

648
00:39:56,761 --> 00:40:00,374
Yeah. Uh, in an
attempt to, uh, obtain

649
00:40:00,398 --> 00:40:03,478
a very important contract
with the Pentagon.

650
00:40:03,502 --> 00:40:04,578
Yes.

651
00:40:04,602 --> 00:40:07,548
That would be a
very lucrative contract

652
00:40:07,572 --> 00:40:09,149
for Drake Industries,
wouldn't it?

653
00:40:10,174 --> 00:40:11,985
I suppose so.

654
00:40:12,009 --> 00:40:16,424
And that may be why
Drake Industries paid you

655
00:40:16,448 --> 00:40:19,861
to alter this measuring device
and falsify those test results

656
00:40:19,885 --> 00:40:22,095
to be sure they got
that contract, right?

657
00:40:22,119 --> 00:40:23,597
Objection.

658
00:40:23,621 --> 00:40:24,732
Speculation, Your Honor.

659
00:40:24,756 --> 00:40:26,300
There's no
foundation whatsoever.

660
00:40:26,324 --> 00:40:27,568
Sustained.

661
00:40:27,592 --> 00:40:29,937
Mr. Matlock, in my courtroom

662
00:40:29,961 --> 00:40:31,505
we first present the evidence

663
00:40:31,529 --> 00:40:33,174
and then we draw
the conclusions.

664
00:40:33,198 --> 00:40:34,308
Do I make myself clear?

665
00:40:34,332 --> 00:40:36,644
Your Honor, I just asked
the man a question.

666
00:40:36,668 --> 00:40:38,879
Yes, and it called
for a conclusion.

667
00:40:38,903 --> 00:40:41,048
Yes, ma'am.

668
00:40:41,072 --> 00:40:45,019
Dr. Barnes, uh, you would just

669
00:40:45,043 --> 00:40:48,522
forward your test results
straight to the Pentagon,

670
00:40:48,546 --> 00:40:51,292
uh, before Dr. Redding
returned from his trip

671
00:40:51,316 --> 00:40:52,593
on the 29th, right?

672
00:40:52,617 --> 00:40:53,861
Yes.

673
00:40:53,885 --> 00:40:55,196
So he never would
have seen them?

674
00:40:55,220 --> 00:40:58,032
There's nothing out of
the ordinary about that.

675
00:40:58,056 --> 00:41:00,467
But he came back early.
Did he ask to see your report?

676
00:41:00,491 --> 00:41:01,735
Objection.

677
00:41:01,759 --> 00:41:05,562
Your Honor, where are
we going with all of this?

678
00:41:06,631 --> 00:41:07,942
Mr. Matlock...

679
00:41:07,966 --> 00:41:11,845
Well, Your Honor, at this time

680
00:41:11,869 --> 00:41:14,870
I'd like to make a
somewhat unusual request.

681
00:41:19,511 --> 00:41:23,224
I'm sure it's safe to say

682
00:41:23,248 --> 00:41:26,026
that you have
written and installed

683
00:41:26,050 --> 00:41:28,629
more than a few
computer programs

684
00:41:28,653 --> 00:41:30,231
in the course of
your career, isn't it?

685
00:41:30,255 --> 00:41:32,700
Hundreds of them.

686
00:41:32,724 --> 00:41:36,504
And I'm sure that when
you write a program

687
00:41:36,528 --> 00:41:39,640
it can do almost anything,

688
00:41:39,664 --> 00:41:44,011
including tell a program
that's already in operation

689
00:41:44,035 --> 00:41:47,314
to let another program take over

690
00:41:47,338 --> 00:41:50,818
under certain circumstances,
and no one would be the wiser?

691
00:41:50,842 --> 00:41:53,587
I told you, Mr. Matlock,

692
00:41:53,611 --> 00:41:56,223
the systems program
here is tamperproof.

693
00:41:56,247 --> 00:41:58,292
I didn't say it was
tampered with, doctor.

694
00:41:58,316 --> 00:42:01,929
I said someone simply

695
00:42:01,953 --> 00:42:05,365
shoved another program
into the computer,

696
00:42:05,389 --> 00:42:07,001
one that could
take over the system

697
00:42:07,025 --> 00:42:08,168
if called upon to do so.

698
00:42:08,192 --> 00:42:10,960
I-It can't be done.

699
00:42:14,732 --> 00:42:17,411
You know it can, don't you?

700
00:42:17,435 --> 00:42:19,579
Then where is it, Mr. Matlock?

701
00:42:19,603 --> 00:42:21,549
Where is this ingenious
program of yours?

702
00:42:21,573 --> 00:42:22,783
I'd like to see it.

703
00:42:22,807 --> 00:42:26,587
Well, that's a problem.

704
00:42:26,611 --> 00:42:28,789
Anyone smart enough

705
00:42:28,813 --> 00:42:31,625
to create such a program

706
00:42:31,649 --> 00:42:33,961
has got to be smart
enough to make sure

707
00:42:33,985 --> 00:42:35,763
that no one else ever finds it.

708
00:42:35,787 --> 00:42:37,765
That's your theory.

709
00:42:37,789 --> 00:42:40,101
And I think it's a good one.

710
00:42:40,125 --> 00:42:41,401
I'll tell you what I believe.

711
00:42:41,425 --> 00:42:44,671
I believe that no matter
how smart a person is,

712
00:42:44,695 --> 00:42:49,009
or thinks he is, there's
always someone who's smarter.

713
00:42:49,033 --> 00:42:51,145
Now, the prosecution contends

714
00:42:51,169 --> 00:42:52,345
the only way my client

715
00:42:52,369 --> 00:42:54,882
could have entered
Dr. Redding's office

716
00:42:54,906 --> 00:42:59,086
is for Dr. Redding to have
opened the door for him,

717
00:42:59,110 --> 00:43:02,289
and he contends that
Dr. Redding was alive

718
00:43:02,313 --> 00:43:04,625
when my client
entered his office.

719
00:43:04,649 --> 00:43:06,260
Therefore, my client,

720
00:43:06,284 --> 00:43:08,329
Dr. Bruckner, must
have killed him.

721
00:43:08,353 --> 00:43:11,565
I intend to demonstrate
that this is not true.

722
00:43:11,589 --> 00:43:15,703
Dr. Barnes, would you kindly
open your office door for us?

723
00:43:19,063 --> 00:43:20,196
Door open.

724
00:43:21,566 --> 00:43:26,647
My associate...
has been instructed

725
00:43:26,671 --> 00:43:29,416
how to access
this other program,

726
00:43:29,440 --> 00:43:31,485
the one Dr. Barnes
says doesn't exist,

727
00:43:31,509 --> 00:43:36,290
by using the computer
terminal in Dr. Barnes' office.

728
00:43:36,314 --> 00:43:39,426
Meanwhile, Dr. Bruckner

729
00:43:39,450 --> 00:43:42,463
will do exactly as he
did the day of the murder.

730
00:43:42,487 --> 00:43:44,186
He'll cross the foyer

731
00:43:46,124 --> 00:43:47,924
and announce himself:

732
00:43:49,460 --> 00:43:51,805
Dr. Redding?

733
00:43:57,168 --> 00:43:58,946
What the prosecution said

734
00:43:58,970 --> 00:44:03,250
could not happen has happened.

735
00:44:03,274 --> 00:44:07,388
The door opened
with no one inside.

736
00:44:07,412 --> 00:44:10,024
No one alive, that is.

737
00:44:11,449 --> 00:44:15,384
The computer did three
things it was not supposed to do.

738
00:44:16,587 --> 00:44:19,165
It allowed Miss Thomas to know

739
00:44:19,189 --> 00:44:23,670
that Dr. Bruckner was
outside Dr. Redding's door,

740
00:44:23,694 --> 00:44:28,275
and it allowed her to
open Dr. Redding's door

741
00:44:28,299 --> 00:44:30,099
from Dr. Barnes' office.

742
00:44:31,702 --> 00:44:35,316
This all happened
because of a program

743
00:44:35,340 --> 00:44:39,708
you said did not exist.

744
00:44:46,784 --> 00:44:49,418
It's not possible.

745
00:44:52,457 --> 00:44:54,034
How did you find it?

746
00:44:54,058 --> 00:44:56,336
How did you do it?

747
00:44:56,360 --> 00:44:59,739
You couldn't have accessed it.

748
00:45:04,235 --> 00:45:05,646
This isn't mine.

749
00:45:05,670 --> 00:45:09,250
I didn't say we
accessed your program.

750
00:45:09,274 --> 00:45:14,955
Actually, we
accessed his program.

751
00:45:14,979 --> 00:45:17,958
It never occurred to you that
someone might be smart enough

752
00:45:17,982 --> 00:45:22,551
to create a program that
approximated yours, did it?

753
00:45:23,621 --> 00:45:25,065
Him?

754
00:45:25,089 --> 00:45:26,366
He wrote this?

755
00:45:26,390 --> 00:45:28,369
Well, of course he wrote it.

756
00:45:28,393 --> 00:45:30,571
What do you think?

757
00:45:30,595 --> 00:45:33,040
He's my nephew.

758
00:45:39,904 --> 00:45:41,348
My stuff's all packed.

759
00:45:41,372 --> 00:45:43,906
I just came in to say
goodbye and thanks.

760
00:45:46,110 --> 00:45:48,656
It's been a pleasure.

761
00:45:48,680 --> 00:45:50,758
I've always liked you, Irwin.

762
00:45:53,684 --> 00:45:57,898
Hey, before you go,
take a look at this.

763
00:45:57,922 --> 00:45:59,299
All right. It's a
pencil sharpener.

764
00:45:59,323 --> 00:46:02,302
Yeah, it doesn't work.

765
00:46:02,326 --> 00:46:03,603
Uh, it's mechanical.

766
00:46:03,627 --> 00:46:04,838
I don't even know where to begin

767
00:46:04,862 --> 00:46:06,806
to look at something
that doesn't have circuitry.

768
00:46:06,830 --> 00:46:08,475
Oh.

769
00:46:08,499 --> 00:46:10,110
Oh.

770
00:46:16,807 --> 00:46:19,920
Speaking of circuitry,

771
00:46:19,944 --> 00:46:21,944
what do you think of Cassie?

772
00:46:23,480 --> 00:46:24,457
Cassie?

773
00:46:24,481 --> 00:46:25,641
Yeah.

774
00:46:27,385 --> 00:46:29,018
She's a girl.

775
00:46:30,554 --> 00:46:32,120
You're a boy.

776
00:46:36,994 --> 00:46:39,539
Here's a 10 spot.

777
00:46:39,563 --> 00:46:41,641
Take her someplace.

778
00:46:41,665 --> 00:46:46,713
Get her a cup of coffee
or a bowl of ice cream.

779
00:46:46,737 --> 00:46:52,474
Talk to her about... things.

780
00:47:12,330 --> 00:47:13,862
Uh, Cassie?

781
00:47:15,266 --> 00:47:19,012
Uh, how'd you like
to go out to dinner?

782
00:47:19,036 --> 00:47:21,048
Uncle Ben gave me a 10 spot.

9999
00:00:0,500 --> 00:00:2,00
<font color="#ffff00" size=14>www.tvsubtitles.net</font>
